A||N||12||wxxo||how full of s*!t is my mechanic??(a/c problem)||||1||
Z||000000||wxxo||06-15-2000||01:55 PM||||ok my ac doesnt work...but why? he says that the compressor is seized up on the inside. the wheel on the outside of it will turn just fine. i asked about it and he said the he meant the it was seized internally. is it possible to know this without ever having the compressor out and inspecting it? seems to me like he just wants the 600 bucks to replace it. if youve ever seen my car you would know that i dont have the cash for that.haha good thing that the windows still roll down. does anyone think that if i put a belt on it and charged the system i could get it working?? tia||||reg||1
Z||000001||86SE||06-15-2000||02:05 PM||||They told me my compresser clutch was siezed and I can not turn it an the outside, its locked up tight.||||reg||11
Z||000002||deceler8||06-15-2000||02:59 PM||||Thought I could get by with just a new drier and orifice tube, but when I removed the old orifice tube it was clogged up with metal shavings from an obviously bad compressor.<P>And my compressor still turns easily by hand.<P>Perhaps this is where the mechanic made his diagnosis. You could do the same, provided your system has no R12 in it. The O-tube is in the line that passes beneath the drier under the hood. If you have a Chilton manual, it illustrates where it is if in doubt.<P>Another option would be to just take a chance on a R134 kit and risk the $35 hoping it will work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||06-15-2000||03:51 PM||||600 bucks sounds cheap.  By the time they finish bending me over, It cost me close to $1300.  They replaced just about everything though.  If you do decide to replace it,  don't be surprized if they want to replaced more than just the pump.  All my O-rings were leaking(so they say)so it would have been useless to replace just the pump.  Good luck and stay cool   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smokin.gif">   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||06-15-2000||09:12 PM||||I'm no a/c expert. But I believe the outside pully spins free of the internal parts until you engage the a/c(a little clutch mates the pully with the internals). So it makes sense to me, that the pully could spin freely by hand. If the pully clutch were engaged, the pully would not spin freely.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Linx||06-15-2000||09:57 PM||||Fiero56 has got it right...the pulley spins freely om a fat bearing.  Look closely at the front of the compressor pulley, and you'll see its a flexible plate thats a hair's distance from the pulley.  When the AC is turned on, an electromagnet (behind the pulley) energizes...the pulley becomes a magnet and yanks the clutch against itself...and the compressor starts spinnin'.<P>There's a small nut on the shaft, recessed into the face of the pulley/clutch; put a ratchet on it and turn it cw.  Even in a good compressor, its a stiff turn.  A seized compressor wont turn at all.<P>$600 to replace it?  man- talk about robbery.  A compressor cost $170 at Autozone.  Y'can take this time to do the freon conversion with a $40 kit.  Do it yourself and save a few $$$.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 06-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||06-15-2000||10:38 PM||||Im taking my moms chevy celebrity 88, its a 2.8L v6 its related to a fiero, to the emissions test, which i was late for, 2 weeks.  YES, i passed.  So im going home, and i notice smoke coming from the engine compartment, so i was thinking it was the powersteering fluid getting on the exhaust system and it was burning.  I have a hose leak.  So when i got home, i noticed the compressor looked yellow, and it was very very hot.  I touched it and said OW.  I think the internals froze up or something.  Im not sure if the clutch popped off, cause the pulley does look different.  My car did stall when i was driving but i got it started and i assume the compressor locked up at that point.  Im going to fix it on my own and ask my friend who just took an AC class knows how to do the seals etc...  Im going to buy a new compressor from wrenchhead.com.  <P>I would take the car in, and ask them to discharge it for you, bring it home, do the work yourself, put new seals in, and add some of that new freon.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||My7Fieros||06-16-2000||03:02 AM||||Linx hit the nail right on the head! Thats it exactly||||reg||1
Z||000008||wxxo||06-16-2000||07:14 AM||||ok but from what i know the clutch will not engage without freon in the system. the compressor hasnt had a belt on it for some time so i know that is a definate no no but i wonder if i just had the system charged and a belt put on it if that would work. looks like the cheapest place to start though. autozone huh. ive heard bad things about that place especially when it comes to electrical. i know that its a huge pain to do the whole compressor thing so i think that it would be worth it to spend the extra dollar to do it right the first time instead of having to do it over. anyhow thanks for the help.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||06-16-2000||07:54 AM||||I've never heard a bad thing about AutoZone, and never had a problem with them. When I work with electrical parts, AZ is the only place I will go. They can test for free, have lifetime warranties, and have always have what I need in stock.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierospeeder||06-16-2000||01:09 PM||||autozone, if its same as pepboys,<P>i would ask for a full waranty, i had an alternator go bad and they wouldn't take it because it only had a one year warranty.<P>With trak auto, if anything goes bad, they will exchange it for you, for no cost.  <BR>Both places in my area, wont take electronic parts back if it goes bad, like sensors etc.. Unless you have a friend that works at a parts store.||||reg||1
Z||000011||86se/lambo||06-16-2000||09:08 PM||||well it sounds like you dont have alot to loose. i think i would first get the belt on and then bypasss the low pressure switch( located at the accumalator) turn the a/c on and see if the clutch engages by looking at the compressor. if it engages then i would go out and get the r-134 kit and proceed with conversion. if it wont engage then i would spend the money at NAPA and get a life-time compressor(you dont want to change that sucker again), about $240 and chane the expansion valve and reciever drier at the same time. sounds like alot but even after taking it to an a/c guy to evacuate you will still save some serious bucks. ben there done that.||||reg||6
Z||000012||86se/lambo||06-16-2000||09:09 PM||||well it sounds like you dont have alot to loose. i think i would first get the belt on and then bypasss the low pressure switch( located at the accumalator) turn the a/c on and see if the clutch engages by looking at the compressor. if it engages then i would go out and get the r-134 kit and proceed with conversion. if it wont engage then i would spend the money at NAPA and get a life-time compressor(you dont want to change that sucker again), about $240 and change the expansion valve and reciever drier at the same time. sounds like alot but even after taking it to an a/c guy to evacuate you will still save some serious bucks. ben there done that.||||reg||6
